# DTF tier manifest: generic-concurrent-put-contract (artifact-keeper#4832, #6317)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single.
#
# THE CONTRACT
# A live artifact at a path (and version) is immutable: a second
# `PUT /api/v1/repositories/{key}/artifacts/{path}` is answered 409
# `{"code":"CONFLICT","message":"Artifact version already exists and is
# immutable"}`, unless the repository has versioning enabled (Generic and
# Mlmodel), which appends a revision instead.
#
# THE BUG
# #4832  The check ran before the bytes were stored and outside the
#        transaction that writes the artifact row. Concurrent PUTs to one
#        free path all passed it: each was answered 201 and the last commit
#        silently replaced the content the others had been acknowledged for
#        (16 of 16 answered 201 on 1.11.3 here; with more DB latency some
#        racers got 409, which is how it was reported). Fixed: the rule is
#        part of the row write, so exactly one racer is answered 201 and keeps
#        its bytes, every other one gets the documented 409.
#
# RED image: ak-backend:1.11.0 to 1.11.3. GREEN: an image with the #4832 fix.
#
# #6317  An upload does more database work after its artifact row commits.
#        Under a saturated pool that work failed on the acquire timeout and
#        the handler answered 503 POOL_EXHAUSTED for an upload that was
#        stored, so the client's retry got 409. PyPI's failing step was the
#        `pypi` metadata write, which left a stored package without the
#        metadata the simple index and JSON API serve. The generic PUT failed
#        on reads that only fill the response (download count, revision).
#        Fixed: PyPI metadata commits with the row, and the generic PUT's
#        response-only reads fall back to defaults.
#        THE PRIMITIVE: pool exhaustion cannot be aimed at one step, so each
#        FIX case injects a database fault in this slot's own Postgres that
#        fails exactly that step, then removes it (an EXIT trap removes any
#        left behind): a trigger that refuses this run's `pypi` metadata row,
#        and renaming `download_statistics` around one generic PUT.
#        (FIX)     PyPI: an upload answered with an error stored nothing, and
#                  the retry is 200 with the row, its metadata and a simple
#                  index entry. RED on main: the error left a live row
#                  without metadata and the retry was 409.
#        (FIX)     generic: the PUT is 201 with its checksum and the bytes
#                  are stored. RED on main: 500 for a stored artifact.
#        (CONTROL) without a fault: generic 201 with download_count 0, and a
#                  duplicate PyPI upload is still refused.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: 16 concurrent uploads plus ~25 calls back to back.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
COVERS="4832 6317"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
